<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Ping from './lib/Ping.abide'
import Recent from './lib/Recent.abide'
import pingSource from './lib/Ping.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import recentSource from './lib/Recent.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>A rung sits in front of the operation, decides, and either calls the next one or does not. It
    is declared on the handler it guards, so neither can be moved without the other, and there is no
    route table to keep in step with either.</p>

    <Section title="`Middleware` is one type, in two lanes">
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`type Middleware<Ctx, Result> = (
    next: (ctx?: Ctx) => Promise<Result>,
    ctx: Ctx,
) => Result | Promise<Result>`} />

        <p><code>next</code> is the <strong>next rung</strong>, and hands back whatever that rung
        returns. Where there is no next rung, <code>next</code> runs the operation itself.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Lane</th><th><code>Ctx</code></th><th>Result</th><th>Runs</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>app</td><td><code>{'{ request }'}</code></td><td><code>Response</code></td><td>over every request its file's route answers, before routing at the root</td></tr>
                <tr><td>rpc</td><td><code>{'{ request, args }'}</code></td><td><code>Value | Failures</code></td><td>on every call to that handler</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Two lanes, one type. <code>Ctx</code> is a record naming what that lane actually
        <strong>has</strong> — the app lane runs around a whole route rather than one handler, so it
        has a request and no arguments; the rpc lane is typed to one handler's own arguments.</p>

        <p>The <strong>Result</strong> column is the difference that matters. The rpc lane hands
        back what the handler would have handed back, not a <code>Response</code>, and that lets the
        same rungs run on an <strong>in-process</strong> call. A page rendering on the server reads
        through the handler, so a rung that authorizes on an id in the arguments cannot be reachable
        by a browser and missed by the render. The app lane stays <code>Response</code>-shaped
        because what it wraps is a whole answer rather than a value, which also makes it the lane where a rung that
        must set a header belongs.</p>

        <p>A rung's refusals join the handler's own in what the caller can narrow, so
        <code>rpc.isError(error, 'NotYours')</code> reaches a name the rung declared and never the
        handler.</p>

        <p>A page following a <code>GET</code> is a call too. The <code>GET</code>'s rungs run once
        as the subscription opens, and a subscription they did not admit is sent no productions
        (18.8), so the rung that guards the read guards the follow.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Refusing is returning without calling `next`">
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`async (next, ctx) =>
    (await signedIn(ctx.request)) ? next(ctx) : notSignedIn()`} />

        <p>One spelling covers all three shapes, because a rung returns what the handler returns:
        <code>return notSignedIn()</code> refuses, <code>return cached</code> answers without
        running the handler, and <code>return next(ctx)</code> goes on. <code>return
        refuse(401)</code> refuses the same way, <code>refuse</code> handing back its
        <code>Failed</code> like any other refusal (17.4).</p>

        <p>Two ways out, and they are not the same:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>return without calling <code>next</code></td><td>short-circuits — no inner rung runs, no handler runs</td></tr>
                <tr><td>throw</td><td>escapes the <strong>whole</strong> onion rather than unwinding through it</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A rung that wants to observe both — a timer, a log line — calls <code>next</code> inside
        a <code>try</code>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A rung runs over the route of its file">
        <Example
            id="middleware-stamp"
            title="`middleware(rung)` — one rung in front of a route's answers"
            summary="A CRM behind a load balancer, and the question of which instance answered. Ask, and read a header the handler never wrote."
            files={{
                'lib/Ping.abide': pingSource,
            }}
        >
            <Ping />
        </Example>

        <p><code>middleware(...)</code> is a hook you <strong>call</strong> from a <code>&lt;script
        server&gt;</code> or a handler file, and it runs over the route of the file that called it
        and every route below. The root layout's rungs run before routing, over every request. A
        section's <code>layout.abide</code> files rungs that run after routing, over that section's
        pages and handlers alone — which is where a guard on <code>/admin</code> goes.</p>

        <p>The card's file sits in this page's <code>lib</code>, so its rung is filed at this
        page's route (37.1). <code>ping</code> is the card's private handler, and its address,
        <code>/__abide/handlers/data/decide-who-may-call-what/lib/Ping/ping</code>, is below no route
        of this page's. The rung runs over it anyway, because a private handler runs under the
        middleware filed where its file's hooks are (16.72). No handler wrote the header.</p>

        <p>So a guard on a section covers every private handler its files declare, wherever their
        addresses point. Filed in the root layout, the same rung would stamp every answer the app
        gives — a page, a built asset, the health check. <code>abide routes</code> lists each hook
        with the route it is filed at.</p>

        <p>Rungs compose in registration order. The call hands back the way off again, so a module
        that is torn down can remove its own.</p>

        <p>Around a route this lane has a request and nothing else. Anything resource-aware —
        authorizing by an id in the arguments — is necessarily a handler's own rung, which is where
        <code>ctx.args()</code> exists.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/run-code-at-start-and-stop')}>Lifecycle</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/lock-down-what-the-page-may-load')}>CSP</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`ctx.args()` parses lazily, and that orders the checks">
        <Example
            id="rung-order"
            title="`middleware: [rung]` — a check that runs before the parse"
            summary="The recent invoices. Ask for nine while signed out, then sign in and ask again."
            files={{
                'lib/Recent.abide': recentSource,
            }}
        >
            <Recent />
        </Example>

        <p>A rung that never asks for the arguments pays nothing. The first one that asks triggers
        the parse, and it comes back <strong>parsed, coerced and validated</strong>:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>An unauthenticated caller gets <code>401</code> from a rung that never looked at the
            body — so they never read your schema back out of a <code>422</code>.</li>
            <li>A rung authorizing on a resource id compares a number against a number, whichever
            method carried it. The string/number split by method is the silent one, and an
            authorization check passing on a type mismatch is the failure it causes.</li>
            <li>A malformed body is <code>400</code> and a schema refusal is <code>422</code>, both
            raised where that first rung asked.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The result is cached for the request, so asking twice parses once.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="On an rpc route the body belongs to `ctx.args()`">
        <p>A body reads once. <code>request().json()</code> in a rung takes it, and every rung after
        that one — and the handler — gets nothing. There is no second way in on this lane, and that
        is the whole reason <code>ctx.args()</code> exists rather than being a convenience over the
        request.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/read-the-incoming-request')}>Request</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Rungs run from the outside in">
        <Code code={`app middleware -> routing -> rpc middleware -> args parse -> handler`} />

        <p>Two things sit <strong>outside</strong> middleware on purpose: a <code>CORS</code>
        preflight, which carries no credentials and so has nothing for a rung to authorize, and
        <code>/__abide/health</code>, for the same reason.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/let-another-origin-call-you')}>CORS</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/tell-a-load-balancer-you-are-healthy')}>Health</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`clients` withholds a surface, never access">
        <p><code>clients</code> withholds a handler from a <strong>surface</strong> — an OpenAPI
        listing, an MCP tool list. It is not access control: the http address answers exactly as it
        did. This rung decides who <strong>may</strong> call, the same one that decides it for a
        browser.</p>

        <p>A private handler is the same. It is listed nowhere and still answers http at its
        address, so the rungs over its file are what guard it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/machines/keep-a-handler-off-a-surface')}>Withholding a
        surface</a> · <a href={url('/app/know-who-is-calling')}>Auth &amp; principal</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/app/know-who-is-calling')}>Auth &amp; principal</a> — where
            <code>principal</code> comes from</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a> — refusing with a
            name and data instead of a status</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a> — what
            <code>ctx.args()</code> hands a rung</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
